Document.description_en:
The National Capital Commission (NCC) proposes to demolish two deteriorated agricultural structures located at 3160 Ramsayville Road in Ottawa, Ontario, within the Greenbelt. The site is a historic farmstead visible from Ramsayville Road, Russell Road and Highway 417 and contains several assets, including a farmhouse and barn that will remain. The proposed works involve the demolition of a concrete panel silo and a timber-frame shed/garage.

&nbsp;

The project is proposed on federal lands owned and managed by the NCC and is subject to approval under the National Capital Act.

&nbsp;

Structural inspections completed in November 2025 confirmed that both structures are in an advanced state of deterioration and are no longer viable for safe use or repair. The silo exhibits extensive cracking, material degradation, and failed roof components, while the shed has experienced partial structural collapse and severe deterioration.

&nbsp;

The purpose of the project is to eliminate immediate and long-term health and safety risks, remove non-operational and unsafe structures, reduce liability and maintenance requirements, and support the long-term stewardship and future agricultural use of the property.

&nbsp;

The proposed work includes controlled demolition and complete removal of the silo and shed, including all above- and below-grade components, in accordance with applicable provincial regulations and NCC requirements. Demolition activities will be carried out using standard methods and equipment and sequenced to prevent damage to adjacent structures, including the farmhouse and barn. Vegetation immediately adjacent to the shed may be selectively removed where required to facilitate demolition, including one small tree and localized invasive shrubs. Where required, disturbed areas will be backfilled using clean material (including material generated on site) and graded to ensure safe and stable site conditions.

&nbsp;

The demolition is anticipated to occur in fall 2026, outside the active nesting period for migratory birds.

Document.description_fr:
La Commission de la capitale nationale (CCN) propose de d&eacute;molir deux structures agricoles d&eacute;labr&eacute;es situ&eacute;es au 3160, chemin Ramsayville, &agrave; Ottawa (Ontario), dans la Ceinture de verdure. Le site est une ferme historique visible depuis le chemin Ramsayville, le chemin Russell et l&#39;autoroute 417 et comprend plusieurs &eacute;l&eacute;ments, dont une maison de ferme et une grange qui seront conserv&eacute;es. Les travaux propos&eacute;s consistent en la d&eacute;molition d&#39;un silo en panneaux de b&eacute;ton et d&#39;un garage &agrave; ossature de bois.

&nbsp;

Le projet est propos&eacute; sur des terres f&eacute;d&eacute;rales d&eacute;tenues et g&eacute;r&eacute;es par la CCN et est soumis &agrave; l&#39;approbation en vertu de la Loi sur la capitale nationale.

&nbsp;

Des inspections structurelles r&eacute;alis&eacute;es en novembre 2025 ont confirm&eacute; que les deux structures sont dans un &eacute;tat de d&eacute;t&eacute;rioration avanc&eacute; et ne peuvent plus &ecirc;tre utilis&eacute;es en toute s&eacute;curit&eacute; ni r&eacute;par&eacute;es. Le silo pr&eacute;sente de nombreuses fissures, une d&eacute;gradation des mat&eacute;riaux et des &eacute;l&eacute;ments de toiture d&eacute;faillants, tandis que le garage a subi un effondrement structurel partiel et une d&eacute;t&eacute;rioration grave.

Le projet a pour objectif d&#39;&eacute;liminer les risques imm&eacute;diats et &agrave; long terme pour la sant&eacute; et la s&eacute;curit&eacute;, de d&eacute;molir les structures hors service et dangereuses, de r&eacute;duire les responsabilit&eacute;s et les besoins d&#39;entretien, et de favoriser la gestion durable et l&#39;utilisation agricole future de la propri&eacute;t&eacute;.

&nbsp;

Les travaux propos&eacute;s comprennent la d&eacute;molition contr&ocirc;l&eacute;e et l&#39;enl&egrave;vement complet du silo et du hangar, y compris tous les &eacute;l&eacute;ments situ&eacute;s au-dessus et en dessous du niveau du sol, conform&eacute;ment &agrave; la r&eacute;glementation provinciale en vigueur et aux exigences de la NCC. Les travaux de d&eacute;molition seront r&eacute;alis&eacute;s &agrave; l&#39;aide de m&eacute;thodes et d&#39;&eacute;quipements standard et organis&eacute;s de mani&egrave;re &agrave; &eacute;viter tout dommage aux structures adjacentes, notamment la maison de ferme et la grange. La v&eacute;g&eacute;tation situ&eacute;e &agrave; proximit&eacute; imm&eacute;diate du hangar pourra &ecirc;tre enlev&eacute;e de mani&egrave;re s&eacute;lective si n&eacute;cessaire pour faciliter la d&eacute;molition, notamment un petit arbre et des arbustes envahissants localis&eacute;s. Au besoin, les zones perturb&eacute;es seront remblay&eacute;es &agrave; l&#39;aide de mat&eacute;riaux propres (y compris des mat&eacute;riaux g&eacute;n&eacute;r&eacute;s sur place) et nivel&eacute;es afin de garantir des conditions de s&eacute;curit&eacute; et de stabilit&eacute; sur le site.

&nbsp;

La d&eacute;molition devrait avoir lieu &agrave; l&#39;automne 2026, en dehors de la p&eacute;riode de nidification active des oiseaux migrateurs.

